window.WizeStreamSearchIndex = [{"title":"Watch, listen and make it yours","url":"index.html","description":"Independent Android streaming with local subscriptions, flexible playback, downloads, profiles and optional device sync.","text":"Independent. Open source. Android. Watch. Listen. Make it yours. Your favorite streams, a library you control, and playback that fits your day. All without a platform account. Download WizeStream Start exploring Android 6.0+ No account required GPLv3 Stable 1.41.0 · Release notes · Privacy by design, with clear boundaries Video. Audio. Your library. Project screenshots. Appearance varies with theme and app version. More than one way to discover. YouTube YouTube Music SoundCloud PeerTube Bandcamp And more Built around how you watch One app. Several ways to play. Switch from watching to listening, keep a floating player nearby, or take supported media offline. Choose what works for you. Playback that adapts Main player, Listen mode, background audio, popup and optional native picture-in-picture. Explore A library that stays yours Local subscriptions, playlists and profiles. Organize without rebuilding a platform account. Explore Offline, on your schedule Individual and batch downloads, optional channel downloads and daily download windows. Explore Make room for your preferences Material 3 surfaces, configurable navigation, accessible text and data-only theme packs. Explore Locally organized. Personally useful. Follow what matters Your subscriptions. Your own rhythm. Bring channels together in groups, tune What’s New, and separate a study library from everyday watching with local profiles. Channel groups and filtered subscription feeds Local playlists, bookmarks and smart rules Optional encrypted sync between paired devices See how local profiles and sync work Beyond the next video More of your media, in one place. Read a transcript, revisit a timestamped note, browse a radio station or play a local file. Useful tools, with their limits explained. Learn at your pace Searchable transcripts, timestamped notes and optional study tools for designated content. Explore Discover live audio and TV Radio Browser, community TV directories and your supported HTTPS/M3U sources. Explore Reach your other screens Android TV controls, audio on Android Auto, and compatible FCast/Chromecast receivers. Explore Privacy, explained clearly A local-first app. Deliberate connections. No WizeStream account, advertising SDK or analytics SDK. Optional network features remain choices, and media services still receive normal connection information. Understand the privacy model No platform sign-in required Manual error-report sharing Controls for history and optional services Readable, open-source code Find the right fit Compare approaches, not just checkmarks. See WizeStream alongside NewPipe, PipePipe and LibreTube. The comparison includes their strengths, release context and source references. 05 Projects, with context. Four active projects and a clearly marked historical Tubular reference. “Not verified” never means “definitely absent.” Open the full comparison Core playback Several capable choices Accounts & synchronization Different privacy and storage models Advanced controls Check the exact app and release Evidence Official documentation and source links The handbook Get comfortable. Then go deeper. All documentation Getting started Install the right build, make a first library, and understand release channels. Playback & audio Learn player modes, queues, captions, gestures, 360° viewing and A–B repeat. Downloads & offline Choose folders, batch media, set download windows and manage storage. Backups & migration Move compatible NewPipe, LibreTube and FreeTube data without losing originals. Privacy & networking Understand local storage, optional services, proxies and Invidious trust. Troubleshooting Resolve common setup issues and write a useful, privacy-conscious report. Make room for what you enjoy Your next stream starts here. Free and open-source software for Android. Choose the build that fits your device. Get WizeStream"},{"title":"Features","url":"features.html","description":"Explore WizeStream playback, services, subscriptions, downloads, local profiles, synchronization, themes, learning, TV and radio.","text":"Capabilities, with context A complete streaming toolkit. Flexible playback is the beginning. Explore the local library, offline tools, learning features and optional integrations around it. Release-aware documentation. Guides follow the current development branch. Stable 1.41.0 does not include every recently merged change. Check what is released → Streaming & discovery Playback & audio Local library Downloads & offline Profiles, backups & sync Learning & notes Live and local media Devices & receivers Appearance & accessibility Streaming & discovery Browse supported public sources without a platform login. Read the guide YouTube with Music and Shorts destinations, plus SoundCloud, PeerTube, Bandcamp and media.ccc.de. Integrated Bilibili, Niconico, BitChute and Rumble; experimental Odysee support. Service-specific search, channel tabs, supported public posts and contextual collection search. Optional local recommendations, saved searches, and local video/channel/content filtering. Playback & audio Keep useful controls close to the media. Read the guide Main video, background, Listen, popup and optional native Android PiP. Captions, searchable transcripts, chapters, audio-track selection and per-channel preferences. Pinch zoom, fullscreen gestures, touch lock, session caption timing and non-live A–B repeat. 360° projection controls, a 10-band equalizer, visualizers and optional normalization/compression. Local library Build an organized collection on your device. Read the guide Subscriptions, channel groups, What’s New filters and selected-channel notifications. Local playlists, remote bookmarks, categories, separators and supported bulk editing. Rule-based smart playlists over locally indexed media. Separate supported libraries by profile; pause supported activity recording with Incognito. Downloads & offline Choose the files and schedule that fit your device. Read the guide Video, audio and caption downloads, plus supported full-video companion caption files. Playlist/queue batches, optional automatic channel downloads and resumable transfer controls. Daily download windows with optional charging and unmetered-network constraints. Optional retention for tracked automatic downloads, previews and permanent-file protection. Profiles, backups & sync Move supported records intentionally. Read the guide Encrypted peer-to-peer Device Sync after one-time QR pairing on a reachable local network. Supported profile identities, subscriptions, playlists, history, progress and selected settings. Manual backups, optional scheduled local backups, and compatible migration formats. Download metadata can sync; actual video/audio files do not. Learning & notes Keep lessons distinct from casual watching. Read the guide Timestamped notes and transcript search for supported content. Optional Learning Mode for explicitly designated videos and playlists. Learning dashboard, supported progress controls, study activity and review shortcuts. Optional per-profile difficulty ratings, with local or supported synchronized storage. Live and local media Use sources beyond an on-demand platform feed. Read the guide Worldwide Radio Browser discovery with station filters, favorites and supported custom streams. Live TV country directories from IPTV-org or Free-TV, plus M3U/HTTPS imports. Per-profile TV playlists/favorites, cached lists and available alternative streams. Local audio/video browser with folders, search, filters, exclusions and rescans. Devices & receivers Use the interface that matches the situation. Read the guide Phone, tablet, landscape and Android TV layouts with supported D-pad shortcuts. Audio browsing, search and resume through Android Auto. FCast and Chromecast-compatible casting for supported receivers and stream formats. Optional HTTPS yt-fifo/Snapcast home-audio handoff. Appearance & accessibility Personalize without executable theme plugins. Read the guide Material 3-oriented surfaces, dynamic or manual colors, and light/dark/black/system choices. Configurable home navigation, text sizing, list/grid layouts and tablet columns. Previewable JSON theme packs with supported palette, glass and native listening layouts. Native controls and accessibility semantics remain important when using a custom skin. Merged after stable 1.41.0 Recent work, clearly marked. Next release SponsorBlock contributions Separate opt-in segment submissions and voting, with a private persistent contribution identity and explicit public actions. Next release Full-video disclosures Optional sponsorship labels that remain separate from membership and content-origin badges. Next release FreeTube transfer Previewed subscription and playlist import/export, with supported modern .db and older JSON formats. The separate off-by-default enable switches for 360° viewing and A–B repeat also postdate stable 1.41.0. Their underlying playback capabilities and these newer settings should not be confused. Make room for what you enjoy Your next stream starts here. Free and open-source software for Android. Choose the build that fits your device. Get WizeStream"},{"title":"Documentation","url":"docs/index.html","description":"Fifteen WizeStream guides covering setup, playback, downloads, local libraries, device sync, learning, themes, privacy and troubleshooting.","text":"The WizeStream handbook A guide for every part of your library. Start with installation, explore the controls, and understand where local data and optional network features meet. Release-aware documentation. Guides follow the current development branch. Stable 1.41.0 does not include every recently merged change. Check what is released → Find a guide 15 practical guides. Full documentation search is also available from the header. Start here Getting started Install the right build, make a first library, and understand release channels. Services & discovery Find supported sources, search effectively, and understand local recommendations. Watch & listen Playback & audio Learn player modes, queues, captions, gestures, 360° viewing and A–B repeat. SponsorBlock & DeArrow Choose community features deliberately and understand contributions and privacy. TV, Auto & casting Use Android TV controls, audio on Android Auto, and compatible receivers. Radio, Live TV & local media Browse stations, import M3U sources and play files on your device. Your library Subscriptions & playlists Organize local groups, tune What’s New and manage smart playlists. Downloads & offline Choose folders, batch media, set download windows and manage storage. Profiles & Device Sync Separate libraries and pair trusted devices without a cloud account. Backups & migration Move compatible NewPipe, LibreTube and FreeTube data without losing originals. Make it yours Learning, transcripts & notes Designate lessons, revisit timestamps and organize study progress. Appearance & accessibility Tune navigation, text, layouts, notifications and native controls. Community theme packs Preview JSON themes, understand glass boundaries and create accessible packs. Stay informed Privacy & networking Understand local storage, optional services, proxies and Invidious trust. Troubleshooting Resolve common setup issues and write a useful, privacy-conscious report. Looking for a quick answer? Start with the FAQ, or collect the right details for a useful bug report. Read the FAQ Get support"},{"title":"Getting started","url":"docs/getting-started.html","description":"Install the right build, make a first library, and understand release channels.","text":"WizeStream is an independent Android streaming client based on NewPipe. You can search, watch, listen, subscribe and build playlists without signing in to a media-platform account. Your subscriptions and library live in the app rather than in a YouTube account. Choose your installation WizeStream requires Android 6.0 or newer . The normal release APK supports ARM64 and ARMv7 phones and tablets. The separate x86_64 APK is intended for 64-bit Intel or AMD Android environments, including Waydroid and Android-x86. Channel Best suited to Important detail GitHub releases Installing a signed stable APK directly Choose the APK for your Android architecture. IzzyOnDroid Updates through an F-Droid-compatible client Enable the IzzyOnDroid repository in your chosen client. GitHub Store Browsing GitHub-distributed apps Use the WizeStream project listing linked from Downloads. Nightly Testing recent development changes Separate installation; changes can be unstable. Use the download page for project links. There is no native Linux build inside the Android x86_64 package: Waydroid runs the Android app. A separate WizeStream Desktop project is linked there. Install and open Obtain the APK from the project’s release page or install through its IzzyOnDroid listing. If installing an APK directly, Android may ask you to allow installations from the browser or file manager handling that APK. Open WizeStream. Select the service you want to browse and search for a public video, channel or playlist. Open an item to inspect its details and choose normal, background or popup playback where available. Subscribe to channels you want to follow, then open What’s New to build your local feed. No Google sign-in or Google Play Services is required for the app’s normal account-free functions. Service availability and supported formats can change independently of WizeStream. Set up your library Start with a few subscriptions and a local playlist. Channel groups help separate interests without creating more accounts. Local profiles provide a stronger separation between libraries, such as Personal and Study. Before downloading, choose destination folders under Settings → Download . Grant access only to the folders or media the feature needs. Downloaded media occupies device storage and is separate from your subscription database. If you already use NewPipe, keep an exported backup from that app. Import compatible data through Settings → Backup and restore , then check subscriptions and playlists before discarding the original. The migration guide explains the supported formats. Stable, development and nightly The stable release listed on this website is 1.41.0 . These guides also describe the current development branch. New SponsorBlock contribution tools, full-video sponsorship disclosures and FreeTube transfer were merged after that release. The separate off-by-default switches for A–B repeat and 360° playback also postdate 1.41.0. Stable, debug and nightly builds use separate application identities. Normal WizeStream uses org.wisso.newpipematerial ; keeping that historical package name preserves updates and existing data after the rename from NewPipe Material. Keep updates straightforward Prefer the same update source you used for installation. A signature mismatch usually means the installed app and the incoming APK were signed differently. Do not remove an installation containing important local data just to bypass an update error: export a backup and investigate the source first. Continue with playback , subscriptions or downloads . For a problem, use the troubleshooting guide ."},{"title":"Services & discovery","url":"docs/services-discovery.html","description":"Find supported sources, search effectively, and understand local recommendations.","text":"WizeStream integrates extractor source directly into the Android app. Services provide different kinds of media and expose different metadata, formats and search controls. A feature available on one service is not necessarily available on every other service. Supported sources Source WizeStream access YouTube Videos, live streams, channel content and supported public metadata. YouTube Music and Shorts Dedicated destinations within the YouTube service experience. SoundCloud Supported audio and creator content. PeerTube Supported content from the selected instance. Bandcamp Supported artist, album and track content. media.ccc.de Supported conference and event recordings. Bilibili and Niconico Integrated service support; details vary by source. BitChute and Rumble Integrated browsing and playback support. Odysee Experimental support; expect limitations. Live Radio and Live TV use separate station/channel directories and imported stream lists. They do not turn every directory item into a supported social-platform subscription. See live media and local files . Search the right service Select the service before searching. Use its available content, date, duration, feature and sorting filters. YouTube Music has its own filter context. Supported presets can preserve repeatable search choices for a profile and service. YouTube searches can use custom or relative upload-date ranges, duration limits and supported deeper collection. Larger searches can take longer and still depend on the service’s returned results; they are not a complete database query over every upload. The microphone button delegates recognition to an installed speech-recognition application. WizeStream requests offline recognition, but the provider determines whether it can work locally and may use its own servers. No Google account or bundled speech SDK is required by WizeStream. Explore a channel Channel tabs can expose videos, Shorts, playlists, past live streams, Courses, Podcasts and album/release content where the service provides them. Channel sorting can offer latest, popular or oldest ordering. Read-only YouTube community posts can contain text, images, polls and media attachments. Viewing a poll is not the same as voting with a YouTube account. Subscribe locally when you want the channel represented in your WizeStream feed. Search within a collection Contextual search helps narrow channel tabs and local or remote playlists. Library search can also work across subscriptions, playlists, feeds, history and Downloads. Comment search operates on loaded comments and loaded reply content. It does not search every remotely available reply or the entire discussion unless that material has actually been loaded. Service ordering such as Top or Newest and pagination remain relevant. Personalize without a platform account Optional on-device recommendations can learn separately for each profile. The For you destination, Shorts and autoplay can use supported local signals after opt-in. Pause, reset and exclusion controls let you adjust that behavior; Incognito does not add new learning activity, and the recommendation model is not synchronized to other devices. Saved searches, local blocks and subscription groups provide useful discovery even without recommendation learning. The app does not need to recreate a remote account profile to organize your library. Report service failures to WizeStream WizeStream’s integrated extractor is derived from NewPipeExtractor with later PipePipeExtractor and project-specific changes. It is not an unchanged external extractor dependency. Report failures observed in WizeStream to WizeStream first. Include a public item URL, the selected service, app version and the action that failed. Testing an upstream app can help identify a shared defect, but it does not change who maintains WizeStream’s bundled changes."},{"title":"Playback & audio","url":"docs/playback.html","description":"Learn player modes, queues, captions, gestures, 360° viewing and A–B repeat.","text":"WizeStream offers several ways to use the same media: a main video player, background audio, Listen mode, a floating popup and optional Android picture-in-picture. Availability depends on the source stream, Android version and enabled settings. Choose a playback mode Mode What it does When to use it Main player Video playback with details, captions and queue controls Watching normally on your phone or tablet. Listen Continues the current item as audio with a visualizer Listening without losing your queue or position. Background Audio playback while the app is not in the foreground Music, interviews and long recordings. Popup Floating WizeStream video window Watching alongside another app. Android PiP System-managed picture-in-picture on Android 8+ A native floating window with media controls. Popup playback may need Android’s permission to display over other apps. Native PiP has its own Android permission and setting. The popup remains the floating-player path for Android 6 and 7. Queue, quality and audio tracks Use the queue to arrange several items, add something next or later, and change the order before it plays. Repeat and shuffle apply to the queue rather than creating a remote platform playlist. Quality and format choices depend on what the service exposes. Configure main/popup defaults and mobile-data limits in Video and audio settings. Multi-audio videos can offer different languages or tracks; select among the tracks actually provided by that stream. Chapter markers help you move between sections. Supported captions can be selected by language; the transcript view provides a searchable alternative to reading captions in real time. Per-channel playback profiles can preserve selected playback preferences for individual channels. Gestures and fullscreen Pinch with two fingers to zoom from 100% to 400% in the main player. Once enlarged, use two fingers to pan. Pinch back or use the Fit/Fill/Zoom control to reset the view. Touch lock helps avoid accidental playback gestures. Volume, brightness and seeking gestures are configurable. A vertical two-finger gesture can adjust speed when enabled and when the video is not already zoomed. Phones, tablets and TV remotes have different control and focus paths; see devices and casting . Captions and timing Open the caption menu to select a track. Subtitle timing adjusts a caption track in 100 ms steps, up to 30 seconds earlier or later. Use Reset to return to the original timing. The offset is session-only and clears when the caption track changes. It does not rewrite a downloaded subtitle file or change the source video. A–B repeat In current development builds, enable A–B repeat under Settings → Video and audio . Its enable switch is off by default . Set A at the start of the passage and B at its end; B must be at least half a second after A. The selected passage repeats during non-live playback. Clear the range when you want normal playback again. The range is temporary, not a saved playlist or an export. The independent enable switch was added after stable 1.41.0. 360° video The player supports automatic projection detection and manual equirectangular viewing. In current development builds, first enable the optional 360° setting in Video and audio; this separate switch is off by default and postdates 1.41.0. Use 360° → Video projection to choose Automatic, Flat or a manual projection. Drag to look around, enable optional motion tracking if desired, and use Recenter to restore the forward view. TV and keyboard panning are also supported. A flat video does not become a genuine spherical recording when forced into a 360° projection. Listening and audio tools Listen mode keeps the current item and queue while displaying a chosen visualizer. Settings → Video and audio → Visualizer style offers 15 styles. The visualizer uses decoded player audio; it does not need microphone access. The audio tools include a 10-band equalizer, presets and a custom curve. Loudness normalization and dynamic-range compression are optional and off by default. Leave headroom when boosting frequencies to reduce clipping. Live streams, timers and recovery Full live rewind is limited by the DVR history a broadcaster supplies. A broadcast can end, change formats or stop exposing its archive. No client can guarantee unlimited rewind or continuing access to every historical segment. The sleep timer supports presets, custom durations, end-of-video or end-of-queue behavior, with an optional fade. Queue recovery can preserve loaded queue entries for later recovery when its setting and watch history are enabled. Incognito queues are excluded; recovery is not a permanent backup."},{"title":"SponsorBlock & DeArrow","url":"docs/sponsorblock.html","description":"Choose community features deliberately and understand contributions and privacy.","text":"SponsorBlock, DeArrow and dislike information are optional community services. They are independent of a media platform’s own metadata and each introduces a network request to its provider when used. You can use WizeStream without enabling them. SponsorBlock playback controls The SponsorBlock master setting is off by default . Once enabled, category settings let you choose how supported segments are skipped or marked. Colors, notifications, seek-bar markers and manual actions help distinguish a segment from ordinary playback controls. Community segment data can be incomplete or inaccurate. Inspect what a category means before enabling automatic skipping, especially if you prefer to hear context, introductions or non-sponsored material. For supported newly completed full downloads, available SponsorBlock segment data can accompany the offline record. Offline skipping changes the player’s position; it does not cut or rewrite the media file. Full-video sponsorship disclosures Merged after stable 1.41.0: current development builds can show full-video sponsorship labels for fully sponsored videos, exclusive/subsidized access and unpaid/self-promotion. These labels are separate from Members only and AI/content-origin disclosures. They describe community-supplied sponsorship information, not a guarantee about the creator or video. Full-video sponsorship labels is enabled within SponsorBlock by default, but remains dependent on the SponsorBlock master switch. Since the master is off by default, this does not mean every installation contacts SponsorBlock immediately. Enable contribution tools deliberately Merged after stable 1.41.0: submissions and voting require a separate opt-in. Open SponsorBlock settings and enable Enable contribution tools . Read the contribution privacy notice before confirming. Use the contribution action in the player to create a segment or vote on an existing one. For a new segment, set the start, end and category, then review the proposed submission. Explicitly submit it. For existing segments, choose an upvote, downvote or undo action. Ordinary range segments require valid start/end times. Highlights use a start timestamp rather than a normal range. Tools apply to eligible non-live YouTube streams, not arbitrary local files or downloaded segment URLs. Contribution privacy and failures Contributions are not anonymous. SponsorBlock receives normal connection information and a persistent private contribution ID. Accepted contributions become public community data. The private ID is stored separately from normal app data and is excluded from WizeStream backups and Device Sync. Keep it private rather than posting it in a bug report. An uncertain response is not automatically replayed as another public submission. Check the service before retrying, because a request can arrive even when its confirmation is lost. The same caution applies when you are unsure whether a vote was accepted. DeArrow titles and thumbnails Open Settings → SponsorBlock → DeArrow . DeArrow is disabled by default. Once enabled, accepted title and thumbnail replacements can be controlled separately. If no accepted replacement exists or a request fails, WizeStream keeps the original metadata. The current reviewed WizeStream implementation displays DeArrow contributions; it does not provide the DeArrow contribution interface available in LibreTube. Dislikes and source metadata Optional dislike information is community-derived and may differ from historical platform counts. It is not an official private YouTube statistic. Media services, comment authors and third-party providers can also change or remove information over time. For a side-by-side view of these capabilities, use the competitor comparison . For data-flow details and controls, see privacy and networking ."},{"title":"TV, Auto & casting","url":"docs/devices.html","description":"Use Android TV controls, audio on Android Auto, and compatible receivers.","text":"WizeStream adapts its controls to phones, tablets and Android TV, and offers audio-oriented Android Auto integration. Casting and home-audio handoff send media information to another device or server; they are different from displaying the Android app itself on that device. Phones, tablets and TV controls Phone layouts prioritize direct touch controls. Tablet and landscape layouts can provide more room for lists, navigation rails and adaptive settings panes. Grid columns, title sizes and navigation choices can be adjusted in appearance settings. On Android TV, use D-pad focus and playback shortcuts instead of assuming every touch gesture has an identical remote action. Miniplayer actions and direct seeking have their own focus paths. If you report a TV problem, include the TV model, Android version, remote action and whether the player was fullscreen. Android Auto is audio-first WizeStream provides audio browsing, supported voice search and playback resume on Android Auto. It does not present a video player or visualizer on the driving surface. Install a trusted WizeStream build and open it on the phone at least once. Connect the phone to Android Auto using your normal connection method. Look for WizeStream in the media-app launcher and start supported audio content. If a sideloaded, debug or nightly build is hidden, inspect Android Auto’s developer-only Unknown sources setting. Android Auto’s Unknown sources setting is distinct from Android’s permission to install an APK. Developer settings are not normally needed just to play audio from a recognized supported installation. Check the linked project guide before changing them. Cast to a receiver Casting is supported on Android 8 and newer with compatible FCast and Chromecast-compatible receivers on the same local network. It does not require Google Play Services for WizeStream’s discovery and casting path. Open a compatible stream’s details and choose Cast to TV . Select a receiver, then use the available play, pause, stop and receiver-switching actions. The receiver requests the selected media from its original service. Some videos expose no compatible castable URL or format, and a receiver may support fewer codecs than your phone. Kodi and external-player handoffs are separate options rather than the same casting protocol. Local-network checks Guest Wi-Fi, client isolation, VPN routing and firewalls can prevent discovery even when both devices appear connected. Confirm local reachability before assuming the media source itself has failed. Device Sync also uses the local network, but it transfers supported library records rather than controlling a TV player. Its setup is covered in profiles and synchronization . Home audio with yt-fifo and Snapcast You can optionally send a stream link to your own yt-fifo server for a Snapcast home-audio setup. Configure Home audio server (yt-fifo) in Video and audio settings. A trusted HTTPS endpoint is required; leaving the setting empty hides the actions. Use Play now or the queue action from a stream menu or Cast picker. The link is handed to the server; local playback continues, so pause it separately if both outputs would be audible. Keep the server on a trusted home network or a suitably protected VPN. When delivery is uncertain, check the server queue before retrying: automatically replaying an ambiguous request could queue the same item twice. Floating playback permissions The WizeStream popup needs overlay access where Android requires it. Native Android PiP is a separate Android 8+ feature with its own app/system controls. Grant a permission for the floating mode you actually intend to use rather than treating all display permissions as interchangeable."},{"title":"Radio, Live TV & local media","url":"docs/live-media.html","description":"Browse stations, import M3U sources and play files on your device.","text":"Live TV, internet radio and local media extend WizeStream beyond on-demand platform videos. They use different sources and storage models, so their availability, favorites and backup behavior should be understood separately. Discover internet radio Open Live Radio from the drawer to browse Radio Browser stations. Search by station name and narrow the directory by country, language or genre. Save favorites or add a custom supported HTTPS audio/HLS stream. The miniplayer can show a station logo and details with playback and queue actions. Filters are remembered per profile and can synchronize when the Device Sync Filters category is enabled. Station-supplied song metadata may be recorded in profile history when history recording is enabled. Selecting such an entry tunes the station again; it does not replay the song as it sounded at that earlier time. Incognito and history controls still apply. Browse Live TV Open Live TV in the navigation drawer. Select Browse channels . Choose the default IPTV-org source or optional Free-TV source. Find a country, load its channels, and select a supported stream. Save favorites or narrow the list with search and group filters. The directories are community-maintained collections of external streams. Country lists do not require location permission. Availability can change, be region-limited, or depend on the broadcaster. The presence of a directory entry is not a WizeStream guarantee that it will work everywhere. Import your own TV source Import a local M3U file, add a supported HTTPS playlist URL, or use a custom supported channel/HLS URL. Only supported HTTPS entries are imported from the supplied directories. Current limits include a 4 MiB import, ten playlists and 5,000 cached stream URLs per profile. Large or unsupported input is not an unlimited channel database. Playlists and favorites are stored separately per profile. Keep the original M3U file and source URLs: do not assume every Live TV record is included in a portable app backup. Refresh and choose another stream Saved country lists can appear from cache. When Live TV opens or resumes, lists older than 24 hours refresh automatically, retaining cached entries if refresh fails. Playlist management also offers manual refresh. Directory duplicates can be combined by channel within a country. Long-press a channel and use Choose stream when alternative URLs exist. Try a listed alternative rather than repeatedly restarting the same unavailable endpoint. Playback uses WizeStream’s existing player with supported fullscreen and TV remote controls. Service-specific social features such as comments are not automatically available for a plain IPTV stream. Browse media on your device Local Media reads audio and video files to which Android has granted the app access. Browse folders, use search and filters, inspect metadata and thumbnails, and rescan when your storage changes. Exclusions help keep unwanted folders out of the view. Local files can use supported main, background and popup playback. A file must still be accessible at its saved location; moving it, revoking folder access or removing external storage can make a library entry unavailable. Privacy and storage boundaries Directory browsing contacts its directory provider; starting a station or channel contacts the chosen stream source. Local playback does not turn a local file into a cloud upload. A stream address may still contain credentials supplied by its owner, so keep private playlist URLs out of public reports. Back up irreplaceable local media as files. Database exports and Device Sync metadata do not transfer the audio or video itself."},{"title":"Subscriptions & playlists","url":"docs/subscriptions.html","description":"Organize local groups, tune What’s New and manage smart playlists.","text":"Subscriptions are stored locally in WizeStream. Following a channel does not subscribe your Google account, notify the uploader or require a platform login. Your local feed depends on the channel information the selected service makes available. Follow channels and make groups Open a supported channel and select Subscribe. Open your subscriptions to organize the channel into a group. Use groups to separate interests such as Music, Technology or Study. Open What’s New to see the subscription feed for the chosen scope. Subscriptions and feed groups are scoped to the active local profile. Grouping is useful within a profile; separate profiles also isolate library data such as history and playlists. See profiles and synchronization . Understand the feed scope The What’s New toolbar can change the selected channel group and service scope. Switching groups uses cached feed data rather than automatically requesting every channel again. A publication-date cutoff and content/watch-state filters can further narrow the displayed results. Control What to check when an item is missing Group Is the channel in the selected group? Service Are you showing its platform or all supported platforms? Content type Are Videos, Shorts, Live or other types hidden? Watch state Does an unwatched or partially watched filter exclude it? Date cutoff Is the upload older than the selected cutoff? Blocking Is a video, channel, keyword or membership rule hiding it? Feeds are not unlimited historical archives. A refresh can improve freshness without guaranteeing that every older channel upload is collected. Refresh and notifications Refresh manually when you want to check for new content. Progressive refresh can show updates as they arrive instead of waiting for every channel to finish. Refresh all now bypasses the ordinary refresh threshold. Channel notification settings can control frequency, network conditions and title keywords. Android can delay scheduled background work, particularly when the app is battery-restricted or the device is offline. A permission or scheduling limit is different from unsubscribing. Playlists and organization Local playlists belong to your WizeStream library. Remote bookmarks point to service-hosted playlists; those playlists can change independently. Categories, named separators and bulk actions help organize larger collections. Use Edit mode for multi-selection and bulk actions. Where enabled, swipe-to-remove in a local playlist’s Edit mode offers Undo. Review the selected scope before a bulk move or unsubscribe action. Smart playlists and saved searches Smart playlists work with content already indexed from feeds, playlists and history. Rules can consider watched state, duration, title keywords, channels and Learning content. Refresh feeds to discover new uploads before expecting them to match a rule. The current implementation allows up to 50 rule sets per profile and previews up to 1,000 newest matches. A smart playlist is not a server-side search over the entirety of YouTube or another platform. Saved searches preserve reusable discovery criteria and cached results. They are useful alongside subscriptions when you want to revisit a topic rather than follow one channel. Content controls Long-press a video to block the video or its channel. Settings → Content → Blocked content contains local rules and controls. Optional advanced regular expressions and comment filters provide more precise matching; these are local filters, not reports sent to a platform. If a public upload is still missing after checking filters and refreshing, include its public video URL, channel URL, installed WizeStream version and feed scope in a bug report. Never attach a private backup database to a public issue without reviewing its contents."},{"title":"Downloads & offline","url":"docs/downloads.html","description":"Choose folders, batch media, set download windows and manage storage.","text":"Downloads are separate from streaming caches and subscription records. Choose where files should be saved and which video, audio or caption formats you need. A downloaded item remains subject to the storage permissions and file access available on that Android device. Choose folders first Open Settings → Download and choose default video and audio folders. Android may ask you to grant access through its document picker. Keep enough free space for the download and any temporary post-processing files. For batch downloads, turn off Ask where to download and set the default folders first. Otherwise a batch cannot reliably save each queued item without repeated destination prompts. Download an individual item Open a supported video or audio item and choose Download. Select a media type, format, quality and available audio track. Check the destination and filename. Start the download and monitor it in Downloads. Available formats come from the source service. A service can remove an item or change the streams it exposes; a failed transfer does not necessarily indicate a problem with the destination folder. Video and companion captions When supported, a full video download can queue one caption track as a separate companion file . This is a sidecar subtitle file, not a subtitle stream embedded into the video container. Companion caption behavior applies to full downloads rather than segment downloads. For playback outside WizeStream, the external player must support the caption format and sidecar-file naming/location you use. Batch downloads Use Download playlist from a local or remote playlist’s menu, or the queue’s download action. WizeStream can collect a remote playlist before starting the batch and lets you choose video or audio for the group. Each item uses its available default quality and audio track. Optional track-number prefixes preserve a useful file order; duplicate filenames are made unique. Entries that already reference local media are skipped rather than downloaded again. Automatic channel downloads Selected subscriptions can be checked for new uploads approximately hourly. The current implementation bounds work to up to three new items per channel and twenty per check. Background execution remains subject to Android scheduling, battery and network conditions. Automatic downloads are not a promise to fetch a channel’s complete back catalog. They are intended for newly discovered uploads from channels you have selected. Scheduled download windows Configure a Scheduled download window in download settings to allow queued transfers during a daily local-time interval. Overnight windows are supported. Optional constraints require charging or an unmetered network. Running transfers check the boundary roughly every thirty seconds; post-processing can still finish. Scheduled workers ordinarily check queued work about every fifteen minutes, but Android may delay them. Treat the schedule as an allowed window, not an exact alarm. Manually paused jobs stay paused. External downloaders are outside WizeStream’s window controls. Manage offline storage Open Offline library management from Downloads to opt into retention rules for tracked automatic channel downloads. You can keep the newest items per channel, limit managed storage, or remove fully watched tracked downloads. Protect items with Keep permanently and review the exact candidates before a manual cleanup. Manual downloads, imported media, changed-size files and protected files are excluded. Automatic cleanup pauses while the player service is open or Incognito is active. Downloads and Device Sync Device Sync transfers completed-download metadata, not the media file itself. A synchronized entry whose file is absent appears as Not local . Download on this device resolves the original source again and offers normal format choices. The original source may no longer be available. Keep your own media-file backups when you need durable access. App database backup and peer-to-peer synchronization are not substitutes for copying your actual video, audio and subtitle files."},{"title":"Profiles & Device Sync","url":"docs/profiles-sync.html","description":"Separate libraries and pair trusted devices without a cloud account.","text":"Local profiles and Device Sync solve different problems. A profile separates parts of a library on one device; synchronization exchanges supported records between trusted WizeStream devices. Neither requires a WizeStream cloud account. Create a useful profile structure Create profiles for the contexts you actually need, such as Personal, Work or Study, and switch from the navigation drawer. Profile-owned subscriptions, playlists, feed groups, history and playback positions remain distinct. Do not assume every setting is profile-specific. Some controls, learning notes and selected synchronization categories are app-wide. Review the individual setting and the sync category list before relying on separation for a particular record type. Pair two devices Put both devices on the same reachable Wi-Fi, Ethernet or local hotspot network. Open Settings → Device synchronization on both devices. Choose Show pairing code on one and keep that screen visible. Choose Scan pairing code on the other and scan the QR code. Wait for confirmation, then select Sync now for an immediate exchange. The QR code expires after five minutes and is single-use. Successful pairing establishes trust between device identities, so ordinary later synchronization does not require rescanning. Select what to synchronize Category Behavior Profile-owned libraries Subscriptions, groups, playlists, history and progress preserve supported profile identities. Search history Excluded unless enabled on both devices. Selected settings Only explicitly supported or allowlisted settings are exchanged. Content filters Supported blocking and filtering choices can be exchanged. Learning records Notes and supported optional difficulty ratings have their own controls. Completed downloads Metadata is synchronized; media-file contents are not. AiSList synchronization transfers your selected behavior, not a shared copy of its upstream data. Each device downloads its own cache when that feature is enabled. Background behavior After pairing, automatic synchronization can attempt an exchange roughly hourly over Wi-Fi or Ethernet when battery and reachability conditions permit. An optional charging condition can further constrain scheduled work. While automatic synchronization is enabled and a trusted device remains paired, a foreground listener can show a low-priority Device synchronization notification. A temporarily offline peer remains paired; use Sync now when both devices are available. Merging and deleting profiles Synchronization merges supported records. It is not a one-way replacement or a full backup. Stable profile identifiers help prevent similarly named profiles from silently merging into each other. Deleting a non-default profile is a local action. It does not remotely delete every record already synchronized to another device. A local tombstone prevents that same profile identity from silently reappearing on the deleting device. Kids Mode and Incognito Kids Mode uses a local parent PIN to protect settings and profile changes and restrict selected YouTube actions. It does not inspect every video or provide an automatic guarantee that content is suitable for children. Incognito pauses supported activity recording. It does not make network requests anonymous or hide your IP address from media services, proxies or network providers. See privacy and networking . Disconnect and recover safely Use Clear trusted devices to remove pairings. Devices must pair again before exchanging data. Maintain an independent portable backup through Settings → Backup and restore ; a synchronized deletion or unwanted merge can propagate to peers, so sync is not your recovery history. If pairing fails, check local-network reachability, guest-network isolation, camera permission, expired QR codes and Android battery restrictions. Do not expose the synchronization listener to the public internet as a workaround."},{"title":"Backups & migration","url":"docs/backups-migration.html","description":"Move compatible NewPipe, LibreTube and FreeTube data without losing originals.","text":"Backups are portable recovery files. Device Sync is an exchange between paired devices. Keep both concepts separate, and preserve your original export until you have checked the imported data in WizeStream. Make a WizeStream backup Open Settings → Backup and restore to export supported app data to a location you choose. Store the file somewhere you can recover if the device is lost or its app storage is cleared. Backups can contain personal library information, history and settings. Treat them as private files. Downloaded video/audio files, local file ownership and all external playlists are not automatically covered by an app database export. Automatic local backups can run daily or weekly to a selected folder and retain the seven newest automatic backups. Android controls exact background timing. Periodically check that recent files actually exist and that the destination remains accessible. Import from NewPipe or PipePipe Export a compatible backup from the source application. Keep an untouched copy of that export. Open WizeStream’s Backup and restore settings and select the import. Review the resulting subscriptions, playlists and history. The apps use separate application IDs and do not automatically share their private storage. A file from a newer or different database format may require additional compatibility handling; do not uninstall the source app before verifying the result. Import a LibreTube backup Choose Import from LibreTube in Backup and restore. WizeStream supports the appropriate advanced JSON backup, with preview and category selection. The import can merge supported subscriptions/groups, history, playback positions, local playlists and remote bookmarks into the profile captured when the file was selected. Supported Not transferred by this importer Compatible channel and group records LibreTube accounts or credentials Supported playlist/bookmark data Instance configuration and app settings Supported history and positions Downloaded media files and search history The import accepts JSON up to 16 MiB, not arbitrary ZIP archives or SQLite databases. Repeated imports avoid supported duplicates. Imported local lists use LibreTube naming to help identify their origin. This is an import path, not a promise of export back to LibreTube. FreeTube subscriptions and playlists Merged after stable 1.41.0: current development builds add FreeTube import and export under Backup and restore. Choose a modern newline-delimited .db export or supported older JSON file. Review the preview, select subscriptions/groups or playlists, and confirm the target profile. FreeTube profiles become subscription groups in that WizeStream profile. Existing data is preserved and repeated imports are idempotent. Exports include the current profile’s YouTube subscriptions or supported local playlists in FreeTube-compatible .db form. Other platforms and local media files are skipped. The import size limit is 16 MiB. Import a text playlist In Bookmarked Playlists → overflow → Import URL list , select a UTF-8 text file with one supported YouTube video link per line. Preview the entries and choose an existing or new local playlist. Duplicate skipping is on by default. Supported files are bounded to 1 MiB and 2,000 lines. Video identifiers are canonicalized, with tracking, timestamp and playlist parameters removed. Missing metadata may require internet access; invalid or unavailable lines are reported. Remote service playlists are not edited by this import. Preserve external sources Keep original M3U files, Live TV playlist URLs and important local media separately. Do not assume that every new feature’s data is included in a full app export. Proxy credentials and private SponsorBlock contribution identities are intentionally excluded from normal WizeStream backup/sync paths. Before a large import, profile merge, device change or app uninstall, create an independent backup and inspect it through the relevant app workflow rather than uploading it to a public issue or third-party converter."},{"title":"Learning, transcripts & notes","url":"docs/learning.html","description":"Designate lessons, revisit timestamps and organize study progress.","text":"Learning Mode is optional and off by default . It adds study-oriented organization without treating every song, short or casual video as a lesson. Timestamped notes and transcripts also provide useful tools outside a formal course. Designate learning content Enable Learning Mode under Settings → Learning Mode . Open a video or supported playlist menu and choose Mark as Learning . Use the learning dashboard to continue designated material and inspect progress. Remove the designation later if that content should no longer contribute to study activity. Only explicitly designated videos, supported local media and videos included by designated playlists contribute to the Learning workflow. Removing one designation does not remove another active source, such as membership in a different marked playlist. Use a transcript Open the transcript from a video’s Description area where captions are available. Select a language, search the transcript, and tap a line to seek to its timestamp. You can copy text or create a linked note. Caption data is requested when you open the transcript and is not retained as a disk archive of every video’s captions. The service may not provide a transcript or the language you need. A searchable transcript is not a guarantee of machine translation or perfect caption accuracy. Keep timestamped notes Notes link your text to an exact position in supported non-live content. Return to a note when you need to revisit a definition, explanation or passage rather than searching the whole video again. Playlist note exports support UTF-8 text and CSV. Notes are associated with videos, so deleting a note can affect every playlist that shows the same video. Review that scope before a bulk removal. Follow progress and study activity The dashboard can show continue-learning shortcuts, active and completed playlists, recently annotated videos, study time, streaks and a 28-day activity calendar. Supported playlist controls can mark material watched or reset completion. Choose whether background listening should count toward study activity. Ordinary playback should not become study data merely because Learning Mode is enabled. Incognito pauses supported learning activity recording. Remote playlist learning, review and note membership operate on loaded/indexed material. If more playlist pages have not been collected, do not assume their videos are already included in a local learning calculation. Difficulty and review Optional per-profile Easy, Medium and Hard ratings help organize review sessions and hardest-first ordering. Difficulty ratings do not block playback or completion. The current implementation supports local or optional synchronized difficulty storage. Select the relevant setting and Device Sync categories rather than assuming every Learning record automatically moves between devices. Know the data boundaries Learning-content selections remain device-local. Notes have a separate synchronization control, and supported difficulty ratings have their own configuration. A shared profile name alone does not make all Learning state identical across devices. Existing videos with learning notes can be designated during an upgrade to preserve earlier work. Keep a supported backup before changing devices or making a large library reorganization. Learning statistics are a personal organizational aid, not an independently verified assessment of skill or study quality."},{"title":"Appearance & accessibility","url":"docs/customization.html","description":"Tune navigation, text, layouts, notifications and native controls.","text":"WizeStream uses a Material 3-oriented interface with configurable navigation, colors, typography and playback controls. Start with readable defaults, then change one area at a time so you can tell which setting produced a result. Color and appearance Built-in appearance choices include Light, Dark, Black, Follow system, manual accent presets and an Eye Comfort warm dark appearance. Dynamic Material You colors are available where supported by Android. Launcher icon variants and matching TV banners are selected under Settings → Appearance → App icon . Android launchers can cache icons or apply their own themed-icon rules, so a change may not appear everywhere immediately. Theme packs offer a portable appearance configuration. For their format, import workflow and boundaries, see theme packs . Shape your navigation Choose the home sections you actually use and set a preferred main tab. Bottom navigation supports up to five main sections; larger collections can use a scrollable tab layout. Labels can remain visible, appear only for the active destination, or be hidden. Optional service sections in the drawer and global-search behavior can reduce the number of steps to reach a favorite source. Tablet navigation can use an appropriate rail or bottom arrangement. Make lists comfortable List and grid modes serve different browsing styles. Adjust responsive or fixed tablet columns, portrait/landscape choices, full grid titles and supported text sizing. Title, channel, metadata and comment text can be configured independently where the relevant controls are available. Before reporting a truncated title or inaccessible menu, check the display mode, font scaling, orientation and whether the issue appears with the default appearance. Those details make a UI report more useful without requiring a private database export. Player and notification controls Playback gestures, bottom player controls, notification actions, artwork and lock-screen thumbnails can be customized. Seek bars and feed refresh indicators provide their supported visual styles. TV shortcuts and D-pad behavior are distinct from phone gestures. Configure the controls you use rather than assuming a touch gesture will translate to a remote. The device guide explains the supported contexts. Accessibility and permissions Android caption styling, text size, visible focus and descriptive native controls remain important when choosing a theme. A custom skin should not replace a usable touch target with a decorative image. The home-screen playback widget offers previous, play/pause and next. With no active player it opens WizeStream. It does not use background polling or store listening titles as a separate widget history. Grant optional permissions only for the feature you intend to use: notifications, popup overlays, local media access, or scanning a device-pairing code, for example. Appearance customization does not require a platform account. Find or reset a setting Use settings search when a guide names a feature but the menu layout differs in your installed build. Some controls arrive after a stable release, and phone/tablet layouts can show the same preference in different arrangements. Export a theme or app backup before a substantial change. Theme restoration restores appearance; it does not replace a complete library backup. Use the specific reset controls for the setting or feature you want to restore."},{"title":"Community theme packs","url":"docs/theme-packs.html","description":"Preview JSON themes, understand glass boundaries and create accessible packs.","text":"Community theme packs are data files for supported WizeStream appearance options. They are not executable plugins and cannot add arbitrary Android behavior, scripts or remotely loaded artwork. Import with a preview Open Settings → Appearance → Theme packs → Import theme pack . Choose a supported .json file. Inspect the preview and compatibility information. Choose Apply theme to make the change. Recreate or reopen an existing player if its current view still uses the previous appearance. Cancel or Back leaves the existing appearance unchanged. Export the current theme when you want to preserve it, and use Restore previous appearance when you want to undo the last applied pack. Versions and supported styling Pack version Scope Version 1 Supported palette and appearance settings. Version 2 Additional bounded glass/navigation settings, native listening-player layouts and embedded PNG artwork/icons. Version 2 packs require WizeStream 1.35.0 or newer. Existing supported version 1 palettes remain compatible. Vinyl, pocket and cassette listening designs are native layouts. Their presence is not a promise of an animated or mechanically interactive player. Theme files do not replace the underlying media player or its accessibility behavior. Glass has deliberate boundaries Glass styling affects supported navigation surfaces rather than every card, dialog and settings page. Feed blur depends on the supported layout and runtime conditions. Low-memory devices, an active player, or opaque-surface fallbacks can produce a more opaque result. A safe fallback is expected behavior, not evidence that every imported pack is invalid. Pack limits The version 2 format is bounded to keep imports predictable: A maximum 512 KiB pack file. Up to six embedded PNG assets. Up to 96 KiB per asset. Image dimensions no larger than 1024 × 1024. A combined decoded pixel budget of 1,048,576 pixels. Player icons no larger than 256 × 256. These are format-validation limits, not an invitation to download remote assets from a pack. Keep the entire pack self-contained and do not add URLs, scripts or custom fonts outside the documented format. Build an accessible theme Test text contrast, native touch targets, focus indication, labels, captions and action visibility. Check light/dark backgrounds, a manual color preset, landscape and at least one smaller display. Decorative artwork should not obscure the action it accompanies. Themes are included in supported appearance backups, but are not synchronized as ordinary profile settings. Transfer a pack intentionally when you want the same appearance on another device. Share and troubleshoot Use the project’s theme creator guide and schema as the format reference. Include a compatible app version and screenshots when sharing a pack. If an import is rejected, first inspect the JSON version, file size, embedded PNG format and asset limits rather than weakening validation. Keep licensing and attribution for artwork you distribute. Report a theme failure to WizeStream with the smallest reproducible pack and a description of the affected layout, avoiding private media or account details in screenshots."},{"title":"Privacy & networking","url":"docs/privacy-network.html","description":"Understand local storage, optional services, proxies and Invidious trust.","text":"WizeStream’s privacy approach is based on local library storage, optional features and deliberate data sharing. It does not require a WizeStream account or include advertising, analytics or tracking SDKs. That does not make ordinary internet requests anonymous. Local data and Incognito Subscriptions, playlists, supported profile data, history and settings are held locally or in a destination you choose for export. Incognito pauses supported activity recording, including relevant playback, search and learning records. Incognito is not a VPN. Your media service, proxy, receiver or network provider can still receive ordinary connection information while you use it. Clearing app storage can remove app-private data; a separate export is needed for recovery. Optional network features Feature What leaves the device when used Normal streaming Requests to the chosen service and its media hosts, with normal connection information. Update checks Requests to the WizeStream GitHub Releases API when checks are enabled or requested. SponsorBlock playback Supported public video lookups to the selected community service. SponsorBlock contributions The public submission/vote and a private persistent contribution ID, after explicit opt-in. DeArrow Public video identifiers for accepted replacement titles/thumbnail frames when enabled. Live Radio / Live TV Directory requests and requests to the chosen external stream source. Casting / home audio Selected media information sent to your chosen receiver or configured server. An optional community provider has its own policies. Disabling a feature is different from erasing records already submitted to an external service. On-device comment translation On Android 12 and newer, translation can use Android’s on-device translation framework when the required language pair is already present and ready. WizeStream does not download a translation model or provide a cloud fallback for this feature. Language detection uses Android text-classification services, which can be supplied by the OS or device manufacturer in a local process. A missing Translate action can reflect language detection, matching languages or an unavailable ready language pair rather than a missing Google login. Proxy connections Open Settings → Proxy to configure HTTP or SOCKS5 with optional authentication. Supported new remote browsing, image, playback, download, update and community-service connections use the selected proxy configuration. Localhost, private addresses and supported local hostnames bypass it so Device Sync and casting can remain reachable. HTTPS content stays encrypted in transit, but a proxy can still see destination hosts. Credentials are protected with Android Keystore and excluded from normal backups. A configured proxy failure does not silently fall back to a direct remote connection. Plain HTTP proxy transport can expose HTTP Basic credentials to the network between the device and proxy; choose a proxy and network you trust. Invidious and media-host trust Invidious mode depends on the selected instance and the media infrastructure it uses. Saved instances and explicit per-instance trust for Companion hosts are separate from trusting every redirected URL. Settings → Content → Trusted Invidious media servers accepts verified HTTPS root origins, up to sixteen per selected instance. Do not enter broad arbitrary hosts or unknown redirect destinations just to suppress an error. Direct YouTube requests remain blocked in Invidious mode until an explicit direct-playback choice is made after a failure. Switching to direct playback changes who receives the request. Sync and backups Device Sync uses encrypted peer-to-peer connections after one-time QR pairing. Search history is excluded unless enabled on both devices. Actual downloaded media files are not transferred. Portable backups can contain personal library information even when the app uses no cloud account. Share those files intentionally and review them before sending them to anyone. Proxy credentials and private SponsorBlock contribution identity are not normal backup/sync content. Error reports are deliberate WizeStream does not upload crash reports automatically. Review and edit the report before copying it, sending email or opening a GitHub issue. Reports can include public or private requested URLs, service/language details, app and Android versions, exceptions and your text. Remove passwords, cookies, private stream links and other sensitive information from public reports. For the broader explanation and the website’s separate privacy behavior, see the privacy overview ."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","url":"docs/troubleshooting.html","description":"Resolve common setup issues and write a useful, privacy-conscious report.","text":"Start with the installed version, selected service and exact action that failed. Many problems come from a changed source stream, a local filter, a missing permission or a background scheduling restriction. A useful report explains the observed behavior without assuming its internal cause. Installation or update fails Check the APK architecture, Android version and installation source. The normal APK is for ARM64/ARMv7; the separate x86_64 file is for compatible Android environments on Intel/AMD hardware. A signature mismatch can mean the installed build and incoming APK were signed by different keys. Keep a backup before considering removal of an existing installation. Stable, debug and nightly package identities can coexist, but they are not interchangeable update targets. A video will not play Check whether the public item is still available from the source service. Note the selected service or Invidious instance and whether a proxy is configured. Try another available quality or audio track when those choices exist. Record the app version and exact error before restarting or clearing data. Do not assume a black screen proves a decoder failure, or that every error is caused by connectivity. A fresh debug log can help distinguish HTTP, extraction, codec and rendering behavior. Remove private URLs and credentials before sharing it. Live playback stops or rewind changes The broadcaster can change manifests, revoke URLs, reduce DVR availability or finish the stream. Full live rewind depends on the history actually exposed by that broadcast. When reporting, say whether you were at the live edge or rewound, whether audio continued, and whether the broadcast had ended. Use a public stream URL and a fresh log from the failing build. New live-recovery improvements were merged after 1.41.0; do not assume a stable APK already includes them. A subscription upload is missing Check group, service, content-type, watch-state, date-cutoff and content-blocking filters. Refresh the relevant feed, and confirm that the channel is still in the active profile. Include the specific public video and channel URL in a report. Refreshing is not a guarantee that every historical upload will appear; distinguish a recent missing upload from an older item outside the collected feed window. Downloads pause or do not start Check free space, destination access, default folders and whether the source exposes a supported format. For bulk downloads, configure the default audio/video folders and disable Ask where to download. Inspect a scheduled download window, charging requirement or unmetered-network condition before treating a paused queue as broken. Android may delay background work, and manually paused jobs remain paused. Pairing, casting or Auto is unavailable For Device Sync and casting, check that both devices can reach each other on the same local network. Guest-network isolation, VPN routing and firewalls can interfere. Use a fresh pairing QR code and inspect battery restrictions for background sync. Android Auto has its own app-discovery rules. Debug/nightly or other sideloaded builds may need its developer-only Unknown sources setting, distinct from APK installation permission. Auto is audio-oriented, not a car-screen video surface. Appearance, captions or translation differs Reopen an existing player after applying a theme. Glass can fall back to an opaque surface on unsupported layouts or runtime conditions. Review pack format and asset limits when import fails. Subtitle timing is session-only. On-device translation needs Android 12+ and an already-ready language pair; WizeStream does not fall back to a cloud translator when those requirements are missing. Write a useful report Include your WizeStream version, installation channel, Android/device model, selected service, a public example URL, expected result and reproduction steps. Explain what you already tried and attach a relevant, reviewed log if available. Use the support page for the project’s issue and discussion links. Do not post private backups, authentication cookies, passwords or a private review-management link in public."},{"title":"Compare Android streaming clients","url":"compare.html","description":"A sourced, release-aware comparison of WizeStream, NewPipe, PipePipe, LibreTube and historical Tubular across 35 features.","text":"A transparent comparison Different projects. Different strengths. Compare selected Android features across WizeStream, NewPipe, PipePipe and LibreTube, with Tubular included as a historical reference. Checked 13 November 2026. WizeStream reflects reviewed development code; competitors use the named stable releases. Items marked Next release were merged after WizeStream’s published 1.41.0. A–B and 360° playback exist separately from their newer off-by-default enable settings. Not verified is not a No. It means the reviewed official sources did not establish the capability. This is a feature guide, not a performance benchmark or a ranking of quality. Tubular is discontinued. Its repository was archived in July 2026. Its last release is shown for context, not as an active recommendation. Find a feature Category All categories Services & community features Playback Devices & live media Library & downloads Profiles, privacy & sync Tools & maintenance Projects All projects WizeStream + NewPipe WizeStream + PipePipe WizeStream + LibreTube WizeStream + Tubular Differences only Reset 35 features · 5 projects Scroll horizontally for more columns. On a phone, choose one competitor to reduce the width. Selected Android features; verified releases and evidence are described above and below the table. Feature WizeStream Current development NewPipe v0.29.3 PipePipe v5.5.0 LibreTube v32.1 Tubular v0.28.4 · archived Services & community features Account-free browsing and playback ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ Yes; cookie login optional ✓ Yes; Piped account optional ✓ Yes Streaming services ✓ YouTube/Music, SoundCloud, PeerTube, Bandcamp, media.ccc.de, Bilibili, Niconico, Rumble, BitChute; experimental Odysee ✓ YouTube/Music, SoundCloud, PeerTube, Bandcamp, media.ccc.de ✓ YouTube, SoundCloud, PeerTube, Bandcamp, media.ccc.de, Bilibili, Niconico ✓ YouTube ✓ NewPipe-derived service set SponsorBlock skipping ✓ Optional, per-category controls — Not integrated ✓ YouTube and Bilibili ✓ Yes ✓ Yes SponsorBlock submissions and voting ✓ Explicit opt-in contribution tools Next release — Not integrated ✓ Submission, voting and undo ✓ Submission and voting ✓ Submission, voting and undo Full-video sponsorship labels ✓ Separate optional disclosures Next release ? Not verified ? Not verified ✓ Sponsor, exclusive-access and self-promotion — Listed as TODO DeArrow titles and thumbnails ✓ Optional ? Not verified ✓ Yes ✓ Yes — Clickbait removal listed as TODO DeArrow contributions — Not implemented in reviewed code ? Not verified ? Not verified ✓ Title and thumbnail-time submissions ? Not verified Dislike information ✓ Optional ? Not verified ✓ ReturnYouTubeDislike ✓ ReturnYouTubeDislike ✓ ReturnYouTubeDislike Playback Background audio ✓ Listen mode and background player ✓ Yes ✓ Music-player mode ✓ Yes ✓ NewPipe-derived background player Floating playback ✓ Popup and optional native Android PiP ✓ Popup player ✓ Popup player ✓ Native Android PiP ✓ Popup player Captions and chapters ✓ Captions, chapter markers and searchable transcripts ✓ Captions and chapter divisions ◐ Captions; chapter details not verified ✓ Captions and chapters ◐ Captions; chapter details not verified A–B repeat ✓ Supported for non-live playback; optional enable switch ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Subtitle timing adjustment ✓ Session-only offset ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified 360° viewing ✓ Automatic/manual projection and panning ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Adjustable buffer target ◐ Media3 defaults; no user setting verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ✓ Configurable; 50-second default ? Not verified Inline/fullscreen quality defaults ◐ Main/popup defaults and mobile limits; different split ? Not verified ? Not verified ✓ Separate inline/fullscreen defaults for Wi-Fi and mobile ? Not verified Devices & live media Android Auto ✓ Audio browsing, search and resume ✓ Officially supported ✓ Supported ? Not verified ✓ Manifest integration Android / Google TV ✓ TV layout and D-pad controls ✓ Officially supported ✓ TV entry and controls ◐ TV launcher entry; optimized experience not established ✓ Leanback launcher support FCast / Chromecast-compatible casting ✓ Both, with compatible streams and receivers ? Not verified; external/Kodi handoff is different ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Device-wide local media browser ✓ Audio/video, folders, search and filters ? Not verified ? Not verified ◐ Downloaded-media playback differs from a device browser ? Not verified Live TV and M3U sources ✓ IPTV-org/Free-TV, imports and favorites ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Internet radio directory ✓ Radio Browser and custom streams ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Library & downloads Subscription groups ✓ Profile-scoped ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ NewPipe-derived groups Local playlists and history ✓ Profile-scoped ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ NewPipe-derived library Video, audio and caption downloads ✓ Yes; separate companion captions supported ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ Yes ✓ NewPipe-derived downloads Batch downloads ✓ Playlists and playback queue ? Not verified ✓ Channels and playlists ? Not verified ? Not verified Automatic selected-channel downloads ✓ Optional, bounded new-upload checks ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Scheduled download windows ✓ Time, charging and network constraints ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified FreeTube migration ✓ Subscriptions/playlists import and export Next release ? Not verified ? Not verified ✓ Subscriptions/playlists import and export ? Not verified Profiles, privacy & sync Separate local profiles ✓ Supported library separation ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Cross-device synchronization ✓ Encrypted peer-to-peer Device Sync ◐ Manual backup transfer; live sync not verified ◐ Manual backups; live sync not verified ✓ Optional Piped account synchronization ◐ Manual backups; live sync not verified Content filtering ✓ Videos/channels, keywords, regex, comments and membership controls ◐ Age restrictions; comparable advanced set not verified ✓ Shorts, paid content, channel, keyword, regex and comment filters ? Comparable full set not verified — Keyword/regex filtering listed as TODO Tools & maintenance Learning tools ✓ Notes, transcripts and learning dashboard ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified ? Not verified Appearance and navigation ✓ Material 3, configurable navigation and theme packs ✓ List, grid and card layouts ✓ Configurable player and list UI ✓ Material 3 / Material You ✓ NewPipe-derived interface Maintenance status ✓ Active independent project ✓ Active upstream project ✓ Active independent fork ✓ Active YouTube-focused project ◷ Discontinued; archived July 2026 No features match these filters. Try a broader search or reset the controls. Choose for your workflow Where the approaches differ. Local libraries and a broad toolkit WizeStream combines multiple services with local profiles, peer-to-peer sync, learning tools, live directories and offline scheduling. Check the guide for each feature’s boundaries. Focused alternatives NewPipe offers its upstream experience. PipePipe adds its own service and community tools. LibreTube includes strengths such as DeArrow contributions, buffering controls and optional Piped account sync. A fair release comparison A merged development feature is not automatically in a stable APK. Documentation omissions are not proof of absence, and source support can change after this snapshot. Evidence, not guesswork Official project sources. Tagged documentation, release notes and relevant source files were used to check the matrix. WizeStream’s reviewed source snapshot and individual guides describe its column. NewPipe Stable README and capabilities ↗ Stable release ↗ Supported devices / Android Auto ↗ SponsorBlock policy ↗ PipePipe Stable README ↗ Stable release notes ↗ Pinned service list ↗ SponsorBlock submission UI ↗ TV and Android Auto manifest ↗ LibreTube Stable README ↗ Stable release ↗ Piped and local settings ↗ Buffering settings ↗ Quality defaults ↗ SponsorBlock contributions ↗ Full-video labels ↗ DeArrow contributions ↗ FreeTube migration ↗ PiP and TV manifest ↗ Tubular · historical Discontinuation notice ↗ Last stable README and TODOs ↗ Last stable release ↗ SponsorBlock submissions ↗ TV and Auto manifest ↗ Related active project: BravePipe ↗ . It is linked separately rather than filling a comparison column with unverified inherited claims."},{"title":"Download WizeStream","url":"downloads.html","description":"Install WizeStream for Android from GitHub or IzzyOnDroid. Find ARM, x86_64 and separate nightly builds with clear setup instructions.","text":"Choose your installation Ready when you are. Install the signed stable release, use an F-Droid-compatible client, or keep a separate nightly build for testing. Stable 1.41.0 For most Android phones. The standard APK supports ARM64 and ARMv7. Android 6.0 or newer is required. Open latest GitHub release Download v1.41.0 ARM APK Listed release published 12 November 2026. The latest-release link stays the primary route for newer published versions. Android 6.0+ ARM build ARM64 + ARMv7 Account Not required License GPLv3 IzzyOnDroid Use an F-Droid-compatible client with the IzzyOnDroid repository. Prefer that client for subsequent updates to the same installation. Open the project listing x86_64 Android For compatible Intel/AMD Android environments, including Waydroid and Android-x86. This is an Android APK, not a native Linux executable. Download v1.41.0 x86_64 Nightly builds A separate, unstable installation for recent development changes. Keep important library data backed up before experimenting. Browse nightly builds A few useful details Install once. Update consistently. WizeStream keeps the historical package ID org.wisso.newpipematerial so existing users can update after its rename. Official NewPipe has a different app ID and can coexist. Keep the same source Use the same installation/update source whenever possible. A signature mismatch should be investigated before removing an app that contains your local library. Move your data deliberately Export an original backup before importing from NewPipe or another supported format. Verify subscriptions and playlists before discarding the source file. Know the permission Android’s permission to install an APK belongs to the browser or file manager handling it. Popup, notifications and local-media access are separate feature permissions. Follow the installation guide Other project routes. GitHub Store provides another client option. A separate native Desktop project has its own code and release path. GitHub Store ↗ WizeStream Desktop ↗"},{"title":"Frequently asked questions","url":"faq.html","description":"Answers to common WizeStream questions about accounts, installation, playback, downloads, sync, privacy, themes and support.","text":"Quick answers A little clarity goes a long way. Common questions about installation, playback, local data, devices and privacy. Find an answer 35 answers The basics What is WizeStream? + An independent, NewPipe-based Android streaming client with a Material 3-oriented interface, flexible playback, local library tools and support for multiple sources. Is this official NewPipe? + No. WizeStream is not affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by TeamNewPipe or NewPipe e.V. It preserves upstream attribution and open-source licensing. Do I need a Google or WizeStream account? + Normal account-free browsing and playback do not require either. Your subscriptions and local playlists belong to the app’s library rather than a Google account. What Android version do I need? + Android 6.0 or newer. Use the normal ARM APK for ARM64/ARMv7 devices and the separate x86_64 APK for compatible Intel/AMD Android environments. Does it need Google Play Services? + WizeStream’s normal account-free functions do not require Google Play Services. Optional integrations can depend on Android features or separately installed applications. Installation Where should I download it? + Use the download page for GitHub releases, IzzyOnDroid and other project links. Keep the same update source whenever possible. Why does the app ID still say newpipematerial? + The stable identity is retained so existing NewPipe Material installations can update to WizeStream without losing their app data. Can stable, nightly and official NewPipe coexist? + They use different application identities. Stable WizeStream, its separate nightly build and official NewPipe can be installed alongside one another. Is the x86_64 APK a native Linux app? + No. It is an Android APK for compatible environments such as Waydroid and Android-x86. The separately linked Desktop project has its own implementation. Why does a guide mention a setting I cannot see? + The handbook describes current development code. Some merged changes need a nightly build or a future stable release. Check release availability . Playback Can I listen with the screen off? + Use supported background audio. Listen mode also continues the current item as audio while retaining its queue and position. Does the visualizer record my microphone? + No. The player visualizer uses decoded media audio and does not require microphone access. How do popup and Android PiP differ? + Popup is WizeStream’s floating player. Native PiP is a separate Android 8+ system feature. They have different settings and permissions. Can I repeat a passage or adjust captions? + Supported non-live playback offers A–B repeat and session-only subtitle timing. The newer independent A–B enable switch is off by default and postdates 1.41.0. See playback . Is every video available in 360°? + No. Genuine spherical content and correct projection information are needed for a meaningful 360° experience. Forcing a flat video into a projection does not create a spherical recording. Can I rewind every live stream? + Only as far as the broadcaster’s available DVR history allows. A stream can end, change formats or stop serving historical segments. Your library Do subscriptions change my YouTube account? + No. They are stored locally in WizeStream. You do not need to sign in to maintain a local subscription library. Why is an upload missing from What’s New? + Check group, service, content/watch-state, date and blocking filters, then refresh. Feeds are not unlimited historical archives. Use the feed guide . Can I import a NewPipe backup? + Compatible backups are supported through Settings → Backup and restore. Keep the original export until you have verified the imported result. Does FreeTube transfer work in stable 1.41.0? + The new FreeTube subscription/playlist import and export was merged after that release. Use an appropriate newer build and the migration guide . Does Device Sync copy video files? + No. It can transfer supported completed-download metadata. Missing media remains Not local and must be fetched again or copied separately. Is sync a backup? + No. Synchronization merges supported records between trusted devices. Keep an independent export for portable recovery. Are all settings separated by profile? + No. Profiles separate supported library records, but some settings and data categories remain app-wide. Review the relevant guide and sync controls. Downloads Are companion captions embedded in the video? + No. The supported companion-caption workflow queues a separate sidecar subtitle file with a full video download. Why did a scheduled download not start exactly on time? + A download window permits transfers during an interval; it is not an exact alarm. Android scheduling, battery, network and charging constraints can delay work. Can offline cleanup remove every file on my phone? + The optional retention workflow is limited to tracked automatic downloads and excludes supported protected, manual/imported and changed-size files. Review its candidate preview. Privacy Does Incognito hide my IP address? + No. It pauses supported activity recording. Services, proxies and network providers can still receive normal connection information. Are SponsorBlock contributions anonymous? + No. They are explicit public community actions using a persistent private contribution identity, with normal network connection information. The ID is excluded from normal WizeStream backups and Device Sync. Why is Translate missing on a comment? + The feature needs Android 12+, an already-ready on-device language pair, and suitable language detection. WizeStream does not download a model or fall back to cloud translation. Does this website track documentation searches? + Search runs in your browser and sends no query to a search server. This site includes no analytics, third-party fonts or tracking cookies. A chosen light/dark preference may be stored locally. Devices & themes Does Android Auto show videos? + No. WizeStream’s Auto integration is audio-oriented. Debug or nightly builds can also be subject to Android Auto’s app-discovery rules. Why can a cast receiver fail on a video that plays on my phone? + A receiver can support different codecs or require a compatible source URL. Not every service stream exposes a castable format. Are theme packs executable plugins? + No. They are validated data-only JSON packs, with bounded embedded PNG artwork where supported. Scripts and remote assets are outside the supported format. Does Glass style every dialog? + No. It applies to supported navigation surfaces and layouts, with opaque fallbacks in some runtime conditions. Static listening layouts are not animated mechanical players. Help Where should I report an issue? + Report a WizeStream problem to WizeStream first, including extractor or service failures. Use Support , include a public example and your app/Android versions, and remove sensitive data from logs. Need more detail? The handbook explains the workflows and their limits step by step. Open the handbook"},{"title":"Privacy","url":"privacy.html","description":"A plain-language explanation of WizeStream local data, optional network services, manual reporting and this static website’s privacy behavior.","text":"A readable privacy model Local-first. Clear about connections. Understand what stays on your device, when optional services are contacted, and how this website behaves. The Android application WizeStream does not require a WizeStream account and does not include advertising, analytics or tracking SDKs. Subscriptions, playlists, supported profile data and history are held locally or exported to a destination you choose. This is a data-storage and feature-choice model, not a claim of network anonymity. Media services and network providers can receive normal connection information while you stream. Incognito pauses supported activity recording; it is not a VPN. Optional services remain choices Update checks contact GitHub when enabled or requested. SponsorBlock, DeArrow and dislike features contact their community providers when used. Live Radio and Live TV browse external directories and connect to the selected stream source. SponsorBlock contributions require a separate opt-in and use a persistent private contribution ID. Submissions and votes can become public. The contribution identity is excluded from normal WizeStream backups and Device Sync; it should not be posted in a report. Voice search delegates to an installed recognition provider. Although WizeStream requests offline recognition, the provider determines whether it can work locally. On-device comment translation has different requirements and no WizeStream cloud fallback. Sync, casting and deliberate sharing Device Sync exchanges supported records through encrypted peer-to-peer connections after one-time QR pairing. Search history is excluded unless enabled on both devices. Downloaded media files are not transferred. Casting and configured home-audio handoff send selected media information to your chosen receiver or server, which may then request the source media itself. A personal backup or imported playlist can also contain private information even though the app has no cloud account. Reports you can review WizeStream does not automatically upload crash reports. Its reporting flow lets you review and edit information before copying it or sending it through an email app or GitHub. Reports can include URLs, service/language details, versions, exceptions and your own text. Remove passwords, authentication cookies, private links and personal database contents before posting to a public project. This website The website is static. It includes no analytics scripts, advertising scripts, tracking cookies, third-party fonts or embedded social-media widgets. Documentation search and comparison filters run inside your browser without sending their input to a search or analytics service. If you explicitly choose a light or dark appearance, the website can store only that preference locally as wizestream-site-theme . You can remove it in your browser’s site-data settings. Ordinary access still reveals connection information to the hosting and network providers; their logging practices depend on the final hosting configuration. Images and interface assets are served with the website. External GitHub, IzzyOnDroid, competitor and project-reference links are contacted when you follow them, rather than being loaded as third-party widgets. Those destinations have their own policies. Useful controls and references Review history, Incognito, optional community features and selected network settings in the app. Keep private backups and contribution/review management secrets out of public issues. Use the privacy and networking guide for proxies, Invidious media-host trust and individual feature boundaries. Read the repository privacy policy for the app’s canonical published policy. This overview was reviewed on 13 November 2026 against the development snapshot. Newly merged behavior may be newer than an installed stable build or an older policy section; the linked source and feature guides provide that context."},{"title":"Support and contribute","url":"support.html","description":"Get WizeStream help, report a reproducible bug, discuss a feature, create an accessible theme or build the open-source app.","text":"Help the project move forward Useful feedback. Better releases. Find an answer, report a reproducible problem, or contribute to the independent WizeStream project. Read a guide Setup, library behavior, controls and common limitations are documented in the handbook and FAQ. Browse documentation Report a bug Give a public example, installed version, expected behavior and steps to reproduce. Report WizeStream failures to this project first. Open the bug form Discuss an idea Explain the workflow you want to improve or join a project discussion. Posting on GitHub requires a GitHub account. Open Discussions Before opening an issue Make the problem easy to understand. Check the installed version and whether you are using stable, nightly or a PR/debug build. Identify the selected service, optional instance/proxy and a public example URL. Write the exact action, expected result and observed result. Include Android/device details and relevant playback or filter state. Review any log before sharing it. Remove cookies, credentials, private URLs and personal data. Do not upload a full private database just to demonstrate a missing item. For sensitive material, ask the maintainer for an appropriate private route before posting it. Use the troubleshooting checklist Ways to contribute Code is one of many useful contributions. Focused fixes and testing Reproduction steps, device checks, playback fixes, accessibility improvements and well-scoped code changes help maintainers review behavior clearly. Contribution guide ↗ Documentation and translations Clear instructions and native-speaker-reviewed translations improve the experience without adding a new service dependency. Explore the repository ↗ Accessible community themes Build within the documented data-only format, preserve native controls and share compatible artwork with appropriate licensing. Theme pack guide Build from source Open code. Integrated extractor. The app and its integrated extractor source are compiled in the same repository. Start with Git, JDK 21 and the Android SDK requirements in the maintained build guide. git clone https://github.com/wizdom13/WizeStream.git cd WizeStream scripts/build.sh debug Read BUILDING.md Feature request form ↗"},{"title":"About WizeStream","url":"about.html","description":"Learn about WizeStream’s independent direction, NewPipe lineage, licensing, integrated extractor, credits and website documentation.","text":"The independent project A familiar foundation. Its own direction. WizeStream builds on NewPipe’s libre software foundation while developing its own playback, local library, interface and service-support work. Independent, with upstream credit WizeStream is maintained as an independent NewPipe-based Android application by WizDom13. It is not affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by the official NewPipe project, TeamNewPipe or NewPipe e.V. Material 3-oriented design is one part of its purpose. The app also develops local profiles, device synchronization, offline organization, learning tools, live media and coordinated player/extractor changes. An integrated codebase The bundled extractor source is derived from NewPipeExtractor and later incorporated PipePipeExtractor and WizeStream-specific changes. It is maintained inside the app repository, rather than being presented as an unchanged upstream binary dependency. WizeStream accepts responsibility for its integrated changes. Problems seen in WizeStream should be reported to WizeStream first; an upstream comparison can help investigate a shared issue without moving the report away from this project. Why the name changed The app was previously called NewPipe Material. The WizeStream name distinguishes this independently maintained application from official NewPipe. The historical Android package identity remains unchanged so users can continue receiving compatible signed updates without resetting their local data. Open-source licensing WizeStream preserves the GPLv3 license, upstream attribution and third-party notices. The repository and in-app license section are the references for source and component licensing. Read the project license ↗ · See the tracked upstream baseline ↗ People and projects behind the work NewPipe contributors and the upstream libre software foundation. PipePipeExtractor contributors and the project-specific integrated service work. Fabián PS (@FabianOvrWrt), who contributed WizeStream’s logo graphics. IzzySoft and IzzyOnDroid for distribution, independent reproducibility work and diagnostics. Community members who report reproducible problems, review changes, translate, test and share accessible themes. Logo contribution ↗ · NewPipe ↗ · IzzyOnDroid listing ↗ About this website This multipage handbook is delivered as static files with locally served assets. Search and comparison controls operate in your browser. There is no analytics integration or embedded third-party review widget. Product screenshots are drawn from the project repository; themes, layout and available actions can vary with the app build. The comparison names its checked competitor releases and marks unknown capabilities rather than assuming they are absent."},{"title":"Release notes and availability","url":"releases.html","description":"Understand WizeStream stable releases, recent development changes and which documented capabilities may require a newer build.","text":"Know what is in your build Stable releases. Recent development. The stable download and the development handbook do not always describe the same set of changes. Merged after 1.41.0 Not in the listed stable APK yet. Independent 360° playback and A–B repeat enable switches, both off by default. Further live DVR protection, bounded preparation recovery and compatible buffered DASH URL refresh. Optional full-video sponsorship disclosures and opt-in SponsorBlock contribution tools. FreeTube subscription and playlist import/export. The underlying 360° capability is already listed in 1.41.0. Its newer enable/disable setting is a separate change. Read the installed build’s release notes rather than assuming all handbook features are present. Inspect current development changes ↗ Latest listed stable WizeStream 1.41.0 360° video projection controls, panning, optional motion tracking and Recenter. Live TV with directory browsing, M3U/HTTPS sources, filters, favorites and alternative streams. Search loaded comments, optional yt-fifo/Snapcast handoff, radio metadata history and playback/interface fixes. Release page Earlier stable milestone WizeStream 1.40.1 Optional Glass navigation styling and supported feed blur. Local Media playlist/tracks navigation improvements. Release compiler reproducibility-related fixes and diagnostics. Release page Earlier stable milestone WizeStream 1.40.0 Live Radio discovery, favorites and custom supported streams. Searchable station filters and profile-aware remembered choices. What’s New date filtering and related refresh improvements. Release page Earlier stable milestone WizeStream 1.39.0 Combined content/watch-state list filters. Voice-recognition compatibility and compact landscape/DeX cards. Playback, feed and sync handling improvements. Release page Need the complete history? This page summarizes recent milestones. The repository maintains the detailed changelog and all published APK releases. Full changelog ↗ All releases ↗"},{"title":"Screenshots","url":"screenshots.html","description":"Explore real WizeStream repository screenshots of playback, channels, history and bookmarked playlists.","text":"A look inside Familiar controls. A flexible library. Real screenshots from the WizeStream repository, shown without recreating or inventing app screens. These repository screenshots show a particular theme and build. Layout, wording and available actions may differ in newer releases or on your device. Playback and comments Video details with background, popup and download actions. Channel browsing Local subscription, channel tabs and supported stream filters. History and progress A history view with watch progress and content filters. Bookmarked playlists Saved playlist entries inside the local library. Make room for what you enjoy Your next stream starts here. Free and open-source software for Android. Choose the build that fits your device. Get WizeStream"}];
